// ※ All variable values are managed within the 0~1 range; an increase in value strengthens variable influence, a decrease weakens it. Policy options and trigger events are determined based on whether thresholds are exceeded.

// [Cautions and Limitations for Use]
// 1. Variable values must strictly be maintained within the 0~1 range (Normalization)
//    - Values must be adjusted using max(0, min(1, value)) after calculation.
//    - Clamping is essential when checking cumulative changes.
//
// 2. Inter-variable interactions not included (Additional logic required)
//    - E.g., Possible to automatically apply Tension increase => Diplomatic Support -0.02 decrease.
//
// 3. Trigger and event conflict management required
//    - Priority definition needed if simultaneous occurrences. (E.g., Military Conflict vs. Internal Coup)
//
// 4. Temporal factors not applied (Time Decay, Recovery)
//    - E.g., Possible to apply 5% natural recovery of variable values each Turn.
//
// 5. Complex event processing limitations
//    - When complex events occur, weighted adjustments or a priority matrix for single events are needed.
//
// 6. Variable change rates based on empirical estimates (Future Calibration required)
//    - Adjustment highly recommended after Test Run and Validation.

